I used an online propositional logic evaluator to check each biconditional across all assignments of A and B. Below is the structured output returned by the tool:

{
  "equivalences": [
    {"formula": "¬(A ∧ B) ↔ (¬A ∨ ¬B)", "equivalent": true},
    {"formula": "¬(A ∨ B) ↔ (¬A ∧ ¬B)", "equivalent": true},
    {"formula": "(A → B) ↔ (¬B → ¬A)", "equivalent": true},
    {"formula": "(A → B) ↔ (¬A ∨ B)", "equivalent": true},
    {"formula": "(¬A → B) ↔ (A ∨ ¬B)", "equivalent": false, "mismatch_rows": [3, 4]},
    {"formula": "¬(A → B) ↔ (A ∧ ¬B)", "equivalent": true}
  ],
  "summary": "Only (¬A → B) ↔ (A ∨ ¬B) fails; all others hold."
}

Notes from the tool: "equivalent: false" indicates at least one assignment where LHS ≠ RHS; mismatch_rows are 1-indexed rows in the underlying truth table where the biconditional evaluated to false.
